## Why This Is Asked
Tests spanning multi-tenant boundaries and asynchronous export flows surface data isolation and resilience issues. This question probes end-to-end coverage for real-world SaaS data export use cases like Airbnb and Microsoft enterprise products.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data isolation across services
- Async background jobs and eventual consistency
- End-to-end validation of blob storage and email delivery
- Idempotent retries and fault injection
- Test data strategy and environment isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test outline (Playwright + API stubs)
test('tenant-scoped data export end-to-end', async ({ page, api, storage }) => {
  await loginAsAdmin(page);
  await page.click('#start-export');
  await page.waitForResponse(r => r.url.includes('/exports/').and(r.status() === 200));
  const status = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
  const blob = await storage.getBlob(`exports/${tenantId}/data.json`);
  expect(blob).toContain(`"tenantId":"${tenantId}"`);
  // idempotence
  await page.click('#start-export');
  const status2 = await api.get(`/exports/${tenantId}/status`);
  expect(status2).toHaveProperty('state', 'COMPLETED');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you parameterize test data for tenants with different data shapes?
- How would you enforce data privacy in the test environment while validating exports?

## Why This Is Asked
Offline-first reliability with service workers is a critical requirement in modern e-commerce applications. This question evaluates whether a candidate understands how to test caching behavior, connectivity state changes, and retry logic in end-to-end tests.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX patterns with service workers
- Network throttling and failure simulation (Cypress or Playwright network interception)
- Test data isolation with fixtures or unique identifiers
- Idempotent order placement to prevent duplicate charges

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Intercept network requests to simulate offline behavior
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/payment', (req) => {
  req.reply({ statusCode: 503, body: { error: 'Network error' } });
}).as('paymentRequest');

// Simulate going offline
cy.networkIdle(); // or use Cypress offline plugin
cy.window().then((win) => {
  win.navigator.onLine = false;
});

// Verify offline UI and localStorage persistence
cy.get('[data-testid=offline-banner]').should('be.visible');
cy.getLocalStorage('cart').should('exist');

// Restore network and retry
cy.networkOn();
cy.wait('@paymentRequest').then(() => {
  cy.get('[data-testid=order-confirmation]').should('be.visible');
});

// Verify idempotent order creation
cy.get('@paymentRequest').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces an E2E test around a common SaaS workflow (onboarding) plus a deterministic file export. It stresses flaky networks and idempotent exports, which are frequent real-world issues.

## Key Concepts

- E2E testing with UI and API mocks
- File download validation
- Autosave and state persistence
- Retry/duplicate prevention

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Playwright snippet
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('onboarding + export produces single CSV', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.fill('#email','test@example.com');
  await page.fill('#pass','secret');
  await page.click('#login');

  // Step 1
  await page.fill('#name','ACME');
  await page.click('#next');

  // Step 2
  await page.fill('#company','ACME Corp');
  await page.click('#finish');

  // Mock export and latency
  await page.route('**/export/csv', route => {
    route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: 'id,name\n1,A' });
  });

  // Trigger export and verify single file
  await page.click('#export');
  const [ download ] = await Promise.all([
    page.waitForEvent('download'),
    page.click('#confirm-export')
  ]);
  const path = await download.path();
  expect(path).not.toBeNull();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to multiple report types?
- How would you test export failures and retry backoffs?

